\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {(c)}Simulated vortices and pressure segments}{4}{subsection.2.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the four simulation variants. Two parameters are varied: the projected contour size and the way the boundary motion is communicated to the solver. This results in four variants: the raw variant, using the original image-derived contours and $y$-only boundary forcing, the size-constrained variant, using a fixed profile and $y$-only forcing, the x-deformation variant, using the original contours and two-component forcing, and the full variant, using a fixed profile and two-component forcing. The shapes shown are conceptual illustrations of the projected contours and do not represent the actual simulation results.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:models}{{2}{4}{Illustration of the four simulation variants. Two parameters are varied: the projected contour size and the way the boundary motion is communicated to the solver. This results in four variants: the raw variant, using the original image-derived contours and $y$-only boundary forcing, the size-constrained variant, using a fixed profile and $y$-only forcing, the x-deformation variant, using the original contours and two-component forcing, and the full variant, using a fixed profile and two-component forcing. The shapes shown are conceptual illustrations of the projected contours and do not represent the actual simulation results}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:models@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][3][]4}}
\abx@aux@cite{0}{lagneaux2026}
\abx@aux@segm{0}{0}{lagneaux2026}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of the methodology. (1) Simulation stage: fish-shaped digital twin and cylinder surrogates are generated under different model variants (raw, size-constrained, x-deformation and full). Pressure signals are extracted from vortex tracking and assembled into a data matrix. (2) Dimensionality reduction and classification training: SVD is applied to construct a reduced basis capturing most of the dataset variance. Pressure signals are projected onto this basis to form feature vectors, which train a linear classifier to distinguish fish-like and cylinder-like simulations and define a learned similarity metric. (3) Inference: experimental pressure signals are projected onto the same feature space and evaluated with this metric.}}{5}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:classifier}{{3}{5}{Schematic representation of the methodology. (1) Simulation stage: fish-shaped digital twin and cylinder surrogates are generated under different model variants (raw, size-constrained, x-deformation and full). Pressure signals are extracted from vortex tracking and assembled into a data matrix. (2) Dimensionality reduction and classification training: SVD is applied to construct a reduced basis capturing most of the dataset variance. Pressure signals are projected onto this basis to form feature vectors, which train a linear classifier to distinguish fish-like and cylinder-like simulations and define a learned similarity metric. (3) Inference: experimental pressure signals are projected onto the same feature space and evaluated with this metric}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:classifier@cref}{{[figure][3][]3}{[1][5][]5}}
